WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.600
One changed letter can break a protein.

2
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.960
How can a single amino acid matter that much?

3
00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:14.030
In sickle cell anemia, one amino acid in hemoglobin is swapped.

4
00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:18.270
Glutamate at position 6 of beta-globin becomes valine.

5
00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:22.413
A charged residue on the surface becomes an oily one.

6
00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:27.987
Once the hemoglobin releases its oxygen, it sticks together into long fibers.

7
00:00:29.117 --> 00:00:32.317
One letter changed the shape, and the shape changed the job.

8
00:00:33.317 --> 00:00:36.297
So the shape must be stored somewhere, and stored precisely.

9
00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:38.337
But where?

10
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:39.920
Start with the chain.

11
00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:46.140
Its primary structure is the order of its amino acids, linked end to end by peptide

12
00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:47.140
bonds.

13
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.680
That order is exact, not random.

14
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.440
Insulin, for example, is 51 residues in one defined order.

15
00:00:55.483 --> 00:00:58.083
Christian Anfinsen tested where the shape lives.

16
00:00:58.903 --> 00:01:02.523
He used ribonuclease, an enzyme with four disulfide bonds.

17
00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:05.627
Tube one holds the working enzyme.

18
00:01:06.547 --> 00:01:07.647
Its activity is full.

19
00:01:08.617 --> 00:01:11.937
In tube two, he added urea and a reducing agent.

20
00:01:12.717 --> 00:01:15.257
The reducing agent broke the disulfide bonds.

21
00:01:16.277 --> 00:01:17.397
Urea pulled the chain apart.

22
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:19.750
Activity fell to zero.

23
00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:22.950
The enzyme was now a loose, random coil.

24
00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.510
In tube three, he washed the chemicals away.

25
00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:28.750
No other proteins were there.

26
00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.170
Just the chain, a buffer, and air.

27
00:01:32.183 --> 00:01:37.083
Here is the test. If the cell's machinery stored the shape, what would tube three

28
00:01:37.083 --> 00:01:38.083
show?

29
00:01:43.733 --> 00:01:47.073
The enzyme refolded on its own, back to full activity.

30
00:01:47.993 --> 00:01:51.073
No helper guided it, only its own sequence, in that buffer.

31
00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:53.573
Look at the disulfides.

32
00:01:54.413 --> 00:01:57.473
Eight cysteines can pair up in 105 different ways.

33
00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:02.287
Random pairing would get them right less than one time in a hundred.

34
00:02:03.147 --> 00:02:04.967
Yet the chain found its correct partners.

35
00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:08.070
Anfinsen's conclusion was this.

36
00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:12.510
The fold is set by the amino acid sequence, in a given environment.

37
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.380
For most small proteins, the fold is the lowest free energy state the chain can

38
00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:19.380
reach.

39
00:02:19.633 --> 00:02:21.253
Free energy here is a trade.

40
00:02:22.153 --> 00:02:23.313
The chain gives up freedom.

41
00:02:24.213 --> 00:02:27.293
In return, it gains favorable contacts and freer water.

42
00:02:28.367 --> 00:02:31.127
Keep those words in mind: in a given environment.

43
00:02:32.047 --> 00:02:33.047
They will matter later.

44
00:02:34.017 --> 00:02:35.397
Three questions remain.

45
00:02:36.437 --> 00:02:37.437
Which shapes are possible?

46
00:02:38.277 --> 00:02:41.237
What picks one? And how is it found so fast?

47
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.160
One rule will answer the first two.

48
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:46.360
Geometry allows the shapes.

49
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.400
Water drives the choice.

50
00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.160
The third question needs one more idea.

51
00:02:52.183 --> 00:02:54.603
The shape of a protein is written in its sequence.
